","Vector addition destroys length information.","Going directly is no longer than going in two vector steps."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7b10336fa911537349debe7a"},{"id":"la_batch12_triangle_inequality_vector_norm_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"When can equality hold in $\\|x+y\\|\\leq\\|x\\|+\\|y\\|$?","options":["Only when the vectors are orthogonal.","Never for nonzero vectors.","Only when both vectors have negative coordinates.","When the vectors point in the same direction."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e279c7a2f4535c10c17b40cc"},{"id":"la_batch12_triangle_inequality_vector_norm_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is the triangle inequality considered a norm property?","options":["It says the zero vector has no coordinates.","It makes every vector have length one.","It defines scalar multiplication.","It controls the norm of a sum by the sum of the norms."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c58957420c4bd3afc3781e17"},{"id":"la_batch12_triangle_inequality_vector_norm_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which common mistake overstates the triangle inequality?","options":["Saying the right-hand side contains two norms.","Saying $\\|x+y\\|$ must equal $\\|x\\|+\\|y\\|$ for all vectors.","Saying the inequality applies to vectors in $\\mathbb R^n$.","Saying equality can happen when vectors have the same direction."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d7f7753233da52ffd7e8396a"},{"id":"la_batch12_triangle_inequality_vector_norm_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $x=(1,0)$ and $y=(0,1)$, what is $\\|x+y\\|$?","options":["$\\sqrt2$","$1$","$2$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ba9544e41fa598feabaed076"},{"id":"la_batch12_triangle_inequality_vector_norm_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $x=(1,0)$ and $y=(0,1)$, what is $\\|x\\|+\\|y\\|$?","options":["$1$","$2$","$\\sqrt2$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f4222e15070525765baaa67a"},{"id":"la_batch12_triangle_inequality_vector_norm_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which inequality is true for all vectors $x,y$?","options":["$\\|x+y\\|=\\|x-y\\|$ always","$\\|x+y\\|\\leq\\|x\\|+\\|y\\|$","$\\|x+y\\|=\\|x\\|+\\|y\\|$ always","$\\|x+y\\|\\geq\\|x\\|+\\|y\\|$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c53f25c5a4ce76c8d47c7954"},{"id":"la_batch12_triangle_inequality_vector_norm_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $\\|x\\|=3$ and $\\|y\\|=4$, what upper bound does the triangle inequality give for $\\|x+y\\|$?","options":["$7$","$1$","$12$","$5$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"76c7f32acfd521b5236f73e1"},{"id":"la_batch12_triangle_inequality_vector_norm_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $x=(2,0)$ and $y=(3,0)$, what is $\\|x+y\\|$?","options":["$5$","$\\sqrt{13}$","$6$","$1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8bb384f2864fb75d8f5b6171"},{"id":"la_batch12_triangle_inequality_vector_norm_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does orthogonality usually make the triangle inequality strict for two nonzero vectors?","options":["Orthogonal vectors cannot be added.","The inequality reverses for orthogonal vectors","The two legs add, while the diagonal is shorter","Orthogonal vectors always have zero norm."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e99b08406c1600660242191d"},{"id":"la_batch12_triangle_inequality_vector_norm_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What earlier inequality is commonly used to prove the triangle inequality for Euclidean norms?","options":["The rank-nullity theorem.","The determinant formula for $2\\times2$ matrices.","Cramer’s rule.","The Cauchy-Schwarz inequality."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"40039defa7f8f7113c4d2623"},{"id":"la_batch12_triangle_inequality_vector_norm_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $y=-x$, what does the triangle inequality compare?","options":["$2\\|x\\|$ with $0$.","$\\|x\\|^2$ with $\\|x\\|$.","$0$ with $2\\|x\\|$.","$-\\|x\\|$ with $\\|x\\|$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bd733dd2ad71714c6b465273"},{"id":"la_batch12_triangle_inequality_vector_norm_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $x=(1,2)$ and $y=(-1,-2)$, what is $\\|x+y\\|$?","options":["$\\sqrt5$","$5$","$0$","$2\\sqrt5$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"53d4dd8fdc2c9bb625781b11"},{"id":"la_batch12_triangle_inequality_vector_norm_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $x=(1,2)$ and $y=(3,4)$, which number is a valid triangle-inequality upper bound for $\\|x+y\\|$?","options":["$\\sqrt5+5$","$0$","$\\sqrt5-5$","$\\sqrt{52}-1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9de76d3c7d46e1f2da2d005c"},{"id":"la_batch12_triangle_inequality_vector_norm_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $x=(2,0)$ and $y=(-1,0)$, what are $\\|x+y\\|$ and $\\|x\\|+\\|y\\|$?","options":["$1$ and $1$","$1$ and $3$","$3$ and $3$","$3$ and $1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"37a43babacc90b116e69a60a"},{"id":"la_batch12_triangle_inequality_vector_norm_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the triangle inequality not say that vector lengths add under every addition?","options":["Vector addition is not defined for Euclidean vectors, which is false","Norms are allowed to be negative.","The theorem applies only to zero vectors.","Different directions can partially cancel or form a shorter diagonal."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"91f53f4567d9aad3c9ae02d1"},{"id":"la_batch12_triangle_inequality_vector_norm_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What special relation between nonzero $x$ and $y$ gives equality in the Euclidean triangle inequality?","options":["They have coordinates summing to zero","They are orthogonal.","They have the same norm.","They are nonnegative multiples of one another"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"25eeedc0fc753d17408a40d4"},{"id":"la_batch12_triangle_inequality_vector_norm_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $x=(1,1)$ and $y=(2,2)$. What are $\\|x+y\\|$ and $\\|x\\|+\\|y\\|$?","options":["$2\\sqrt2$ and $3\\sqrt2$","$3\\sqrt2$ and $3\\sqrt2$","$3$ and $3\\sqrt2$","$\\sqrt{18}$ and $2\\sqrt2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"87df0971879098732e58459d"},{"id":"la_batch12_triangle_inequality_vector_norm_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"If $\\|x\\|=2$, $\\|y\\|=5$, and $x$ and $y$ point in opposite directions, what is $\\|x+y\\|$?","options":["$10$","$\\sqrt{29}$","$7$","$3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"08da7c05013f6adc445f2acc"}]}}